New features for Wav Composer Not Toilet!

New features summary:
    * Autogrouping - group modules as they're created.

    * Entry points - a quicker way to connect sequencer outputs to
      to the inputs of multiple modules at once.

    * Nameless data objects - data objects which are not referenced
      by name no longer require naming.

Autogrouping
------------
Autogrouping introduces an easier way of adding modules to a group.
Instead of the creation of modules and then grouping as a two step
process, autogrouping groups the modules as they're created.

    * Simpler syntax than the group data object.
    * Less work to keep track of modules which need to be added to
      (or removed from) a group.
    * Quicker and easier to introduce to or remove grouping from a file
    * Allows further modules to be added to existing groups.

The concept of directives was introduced to make this happen, with three
for the autogrouping process: autogroup_create, autogroup_add, and
autogroup_stop. The name of the group must be specified alongside each
of them.

    // example:
    autogroup_create group1
        constant one value 1.0 one
    autogroup_stop group1

    // something else here...

    autogroup_add
    group1
        constant_freq cf value 440.0 cf

        multiplier
        m
            in_signal1 one out_output
            in_signal2 cf out_output
        m
    group1

autogroup_stop is optional to explicilty specify, but must be triggered
by using the name of the group instead, which makes the syntax of
autogroup definitions in keeping with modules and data objects.

See tests/autogroup.wc for a simple example, and examples/bounce2.wc
for more a complex examples.

In some files, or through your own usage you might come across data
objects within an autogroup section. These are not grouped but do work
with the autogroup in that, for instance, you wanted to edit group1.cf,
doing so within the autogroup you would only need specify cf as the
module name and not the full group qualified name (group1.cf).


Entry Points
------------
The concept of entry points arises from the wish to create a voice part
in a file which can then be reused by multiple other files. Modules in
the voice part connect to the entry point, and then when that voice part
is used, the entry point is connected up appropriately.

For multi-layered voice parts, this concept dramatically reduces the
number of inputs requiring reconnection.

It's not exactly a new feature, the pieces were in place - wcnt_trigger
and wcnt_signal modules... No! No! No! No!

It's not exactly a new feature, the piece is already in place - the
wcnt_note module. The wcnt_note has everything we need all in one place,
which really shouldn't come as a suprise to me as I created it for
the seq_router module.

However, due to the way these modules work (wcnt_trigger actually does
nothing at all in the main processing loop), and the fact I had never
thought to use them in this way, the way the logic of how connections
are formed didn't accommodate all this, which created an error saying
outputs didn't exist when they do!

The fix was to postpone these connections until... they were ready.

